WEBVTT

00:01.000 --> 00:04.160
All right, so since we've learned how to configure

00:04.160 --> 00:07.760
and make Claude Desktop work with brewkeg,

00:07.760 --> 00:12.480
the next thing we need to do is how to configure brewkeg

00:12.480 --> 00:13.560
with Claude CLI.

00:13.560 --> 00:15.240
So I'm just going to be closing this window.

00:15.240 --> 00:18.280
I'm going to split the right pane that's half of the window.

00:18.280 --> 00:22.680
And this is the marketing page for Claude Code.

00:22.680 --> 00:23.800
This is how it works.

00:23.800 --> 00:26.160
But we don't want to use this one

00:26.160 --> 00:28.680
because we want the changes to be permanent.

00:28.680 --> 00:33.080
So .claude, this thing, there's

00:33.080 --> 00:35.760
what we call the config, like claude config or something

00:35.760 --> 00:36.080
like that.

00:36.080 --> 00:38.040
It's hidden, it's in your home directory.

00:38.040 --> 00:39.560
Like you don't see it if it's hidden or not,

00:39.560 --> 00:41.640
but that's just how hidden files work.

00:41.640 --> 00:45.920
So the first thing we need to do is to edit that file.

00:45.920 --> 00:47.240
And where can we find it?

00:47.240 --> 00:52.320
You can find it in the home directory of your machine.

00:52.320 --> 00:54.080
And once again, I'm on Mac.

00:54.080 --> 00:56.840
And since I'm on Mac, my interface

00:56.880 --> 00:59.480
is going to be different, but it's the same concept.

00:59.480 --> 01:01.280
And if you're wondering what this window is,

01:01.280 --> 01:04.640
this is Warp — warp.dev.

01:04.640 --> 01:09.280
You can use your normal terminal, your PowerShell,

01:09.280 --> 01:11.040
your CMD, whatever, anything that

01:11.040 --> 01:13.320
gives you something like this.

01:13.320 --> 01:24.960
So I'm going to open my ~/.claude/settings.json.

01:24.960 --> 01:29.240
I'm going to open this file in VS Code, the editor.

01:29.240 --> 01:33.000
So these are the two things that we need to bear in mind.

01:33.000 --> 01:36.520
The base URL and the authentication token.

01:36.520 --> 01:39.680
Now, if I just open claude without these two things,

01:39.680 --> 01:42.400
it's going to tell me to log in.

01:42.400 --> 01:45.440
So let me get rid of these two environment variables

01:45.440 --> 01:49.600
and try to open claude.

01:49.600 --> 01:51.240
It's going to tell me to trust this folder.

01:51.240 --> 01:53.680
If I say something like, hello?

01:53.720 --> 01:55.240
It's going to tell me to log in, right?

01:55.240 --> 01:56.880
So we don't want to log into claude

01:56.880 --> 01:59.640
because we have a brewkeg account.

01:59.640 --> 02:02.120
So I'm just going to exit it.

02:02.120 --> 02:05.320
I'm going to head back to this file.

02:05.320 --> 02:09.160
And our Anthropic base URL is going to point to brewkeg.dev.

02:09.160 --> 02:12.920
My API key is also going to be my API key that I've gotten

02:12.920 --> 02:14.960
from my dashboard.

02:14.960 --> 02:17.640
So I'm just going to copy it into this field.

02:17.640 --> 02:20.600
So I'm going to edit this one out.

02:20.640 --> 02:24.800
And these are the two variables that you want to deal with.

02:24.800 --> 02:26.920
They are the two most important things,

02:26.920 --> 02:30.000
like line number three and line number four.

02:30.000 --> 02:31.640
You don't need anything else.

02:31.640 --> 02:34.280
This other thing here, these are just like my,

02:34.280 --> 02:35.920
they were added by Claude Code itself.

02:35.920 --> 02:39.360
So once we have this thing working here,

02:39.360 --> 02:42.680
it does all of this to your endpoint

02:42.680 --> 02:44.160
on your own token.

02:44.160 --> 02:47.040
And now when I open claude again,

02:47.040 --> 02:48.760
when I ask it a question,

02:48.760 --> 02:52.920
what should — I'm going to say, build me a website?

02:52.920 --> 02:54.280
Which is a big ask.

02:54.280 --> 02:56.240
So you get the idea.

02:56.240 --> 02:59.040
You'll see that it starts working.

02:59.040 --> 03:01.120
I think my network is a little bit poor.

03:01.120 --> 03:03.920
And I'm just going to wait for it for some time.

03:05.560 --> 03:07.000
So it's thinking.

03:08.800 --> 03:11.400
I can see that it says thinking.

03:11.400 --> 03:15.160
It's trying to figure out what I mean by build me a website.

03:15.160 --> 03:16.720
So wait for it to load.

03:16.720 --> 03:17.880
So it's just working.

03:17.880 --> 03:19.840
So you can just use your Claude CLI

03:19.840 --> 03:21.280
the way you used to use it.

03:21.280 --> 03:23.360
Nothing else, nothing more.

03:23.360 --> 03:24.640
Nothing less, nothing more.

03:24.640 --> 03:26.320
It's just it.

03:26.320 --> 03:27.960
And to prove it's working too,

03:29.080 --> 03:31.080
let's just go to our request page.

03:33.920 --> 03:36.360
Yeah, I think my network is busted.

03:36.360 --> 03:38.400
So in my request page, you see my tokens,

03:38.400 --> 03:39.880
you see the request that I've made.

03:39.880 --> 03:43.840
You see my cache read here or my cache read here.

03:43.840 --> 03:46.520
My input, my cache read and everything

03:46.520 --> 03:48.520
is written right here.

03:48.520 --> 03:52.600
So it asks me a similar question to what Claude Desktop

03:52.600 --> 03:54.880
also asked — a website, an app, a game.

03:54.880 --> 03:57.120
Like what do I want to build?

03:57.120 --> 03:58.640
So that's all.

03:58.640 --> 04:00.720
And you can select the model

04:00.720 --> 04:02.560
if you don't want to use that model.

04:02.560 --> 04:07.560
You can select — I'm on Opus 5, not 5.5. Five.

04:09.360 --> 04:11.720
Five is different from 5.5.

04:11.760 --> 04:13.800
So brewkeg, these are the only models

04:13.800 --> 04:15.360
that brewkeg currently supports.

04:15.360 --> 04:17.560
Note, it's Haiku, Opus 5.

04:17.560 --> 04:19.000
So Sonnet 5, Opus 5.

04:19.000 --> 04:22.040
We don't yet have 5.5.

04:22.040 --> 04:24.760
If you select 5.5 accidentally,

04:24.760 --> 04:28.800
it's going to route you to Opus 5, right?

04:28.800 --> 04:30.480
So that is just the whole thing.

04:30.480 --> 04:33.240
We are yet to get 5.5,

04:33.240 --> 04:35.760
but it's a little bit difficult for us to get.

04:35.760 --> 04:37.960
So right now you can just use 5

04:37.960 --> 04:41.400
and it's going to work — like 90% of your tasks

04:41.400 --> 04:42.680
is going to do it for you.

04:42.680 --> 04:45.040
And it's also very, very good.

04:45.920 --> 04:47.200
I think that's all.

04:47.200 --> 04:48.600
If you have any questions again,

04:48.600 --> 04:50.800
let me know in the comment section.

04:50.800 --> 04:52.320
However you found this video.
